 \section{Introduction}
 \label{sec:intro}
-A robot picking up an apple beside a post faces a problem that object detection alone cannot solve. Its fingers must reach the apple, its wrist must avoid both objects, and the grasped apple must subsequently clear the post. The target identity remains the same while its role in collision checking changes. Treating every surface as an obstacle can block the grasp; removing the target from every collision query can admit unintended contact. Even with the correct identity, an oversized target region can erase part of the neighboring post. Successful manipulation therefore requires answering three distinct questions: \emph{which object is intended, which observed surfaces belong to it, and which contacts are currently permitted?}
+Picking up an apple beside a post requires selective contact: the fingers must touch the apple, the wrist must avoid it, and the grasped apple must clear the post. The same object therefore changes its role in collision checking throughout a task. This motivates a distinction between \emph{target identity}, \emph{observed surface extent}, and \emph{contact permission}: knowing which object to manipulate does not determine which surfaces each robot link may contact.
 
-Vision-language-action (VLA) models translate visual observations and instructions into general-purpose action proposals~\cite{black2024pi0,intelligence2025pi05}. Geometric refinement provides a complementary way to enforce explicit constraints on those proposals~\cite{wu2025momanipvla,hu2026vlsa}. Yet a geometric solver inherits the semantics of its scene representation. It cannot recover an obstacle that perception has removed, nor decide that one robot link may touch a surface that another must avoid. The critical interface is consequently the conversion of task-relevant visual evidence into the geometry used by individual collision queries.
+Vision-language-action (VLA) models generate actions conditioned on visual observations and instructions~\cite{black2024pi0,intelligence2025pi05}, while geometric refinement adds explicit constraints to their proposals~\cite{wu2025momanipvla,hu2026vlsa}. Grounding connects these capabilities: KNOWS uses internal VLA attention for target selection with externally segmented geometry~\cite{park2026knows}, object-focused representations support manipulation~\cite{huang2025roboground,vo2026obeyed}, and attached-object checking accounts for carried geometry~\cite{zhen2026wbag}. However, a refiner can enforce only the scene constraints it receives. An oversized target exception can remove a neighboring obstacle, whereas incomplete target coverage can leave intended grasp contacts blocked. Neither error is resolved simply by selecting the correct object or strengthening the downstream optimizer.
 
-Several ingredients of this interface are established. KNOWS uses internal VLA attention for target selection together with externally segmented geometry~\cite{park2026knows}. Object-focused representations support robotic grounding~\cite{huang2025roboground,vo2026obeyed}, and attached-object constraints extend collision checking beyond the bare robot~\cite{zhen2026wbag}. The remaining challenge we address is to construct the \emph{extent and lifetime of a contact exception} from policy evidence and observed depth. Coarse attention is useful for selecting a target, but is not a metric boundary. A bounding volume is useful for searching around that target, but is not evidence that every enclosed surface belongs to it. Likewise, a predicted task phase is informative about intent, but is not confirmation that the object is physically attached.
+We address the following problem: \emph{given internal evidence from a frozen VLA, calibrated depth, and robot feedback, construct a 3D collision representation that permits configured task contacts while retaining constraints on neighboring surfaces and non-contact links.} The representation must specify both the spatial extent and the lifetime of each exception, including the transition from an environmental object to attached geometry and back after release. The contact links and permitted placement support are supplied task rules; the challenge is to ground and update their geometric scope from observations. Consequently, recovering a target surface and assigning contact permission are related but distinct inference problems.
 
-We present \sys, which places a task-conditioned contact representation between a frozen VLA and an action-chunk refiner. Our guiding principle is to assign each signal a role matched to the information it provides. Noun attention supplies target seeds. Calibrated depth supplies surface adjacency and geometric boundaries. A seeded graph energy combines this evidence with protected support surfaces to recover an observed target region. Centered internal descriptors compare whole candidate regions across time, allowing identity reassociation and consistency alerts without forcing coarse patch similarity to determine fine surface boundaries. This separation also makes uncertainty actionable: an unsupported region need not receive a contact exception simply because it lies near the predicted target.
+Our design follows a separation of evidence. Noun attention indicates task relevance, but its patch resolution does not define a metric surface boundary. Depth reveals observed surface continuity, but does not identify which object the instruction refers to. Temporal feature agreement can support identity association, but does not establish physical attachment. Development experiments further motivate this separation: with improved geometry held fixed, the tested feature-similarity edges and prototype unaries do not reliably improve surface membership. We therefore use depth to determine extent and internal descriptors to compare candidate identities across observations, rather than requiring the same feature similarity to solve both problems.
 
-The representation remains explicit throughout execution. Task-phase estimates are combined with gripper and target--hand relations, while physical grasp feedback controls attachment. Before grasping, designated contact links can query a target-excluded field, whereas the wrist and other modeled links retain the full scene. After grasping, supported target geometry moves with the hand and is checked against the environment. On release, it returns to scene geometry. Constrained chunk refinement preserves the policy's proposed motion where feasible, and a separate prefix check determines whether the candidate is sent for execution. The complete pipeline thus connects visual grounding to \emph{what may be contacted, by which links, and for how long}.
+We present \sys, which connects these complementary signals to constrained action-chunk execution. Attention seeds an observed depth-surface graph, while feature memory proposes identity reassociation and flags inconsistent selections. Task-phase estimates, gripper feedback, and target--hand geometry govern the contact lifecycle, with physical evidence required for attachment. Supported target surfaces are excluded only from the queries of designated contact links; other modeled links retain the target as an obstacle. During transport, the target becomes attached geometry checked against the environment, and release returns it to the scene. A chunk refiner consumes this representation and checks the prefix selected for execution. This preserves the complete grounding-to-action pipeline while making explicit \emph{what may be contacted, by which links, and for how long}.
+
+Our current evidence supports the grounding design at the component level. On 180 selected frames from 32 development episodes with replayed simulation depth, surface grounding improves observed-surface IoU over radius growth and clustering under a matched robot mask; centered feature memory provides a candidate consistency signal. The incremental effect of identity switching remains inconclusive. These offline observations motivate the proposed representation but do not establish fewer forbidden contacts or higher closed-loop task success. The decisive downstream test is whether improved geometry protects neighboring surfaces \emph{while preserving the contacts needed to complete the task}.
