 Our method makes three connected contributions:
 \begin{itemize}
-\item \textbf{Observed-surface grounding from a frozen policy.} We construct target extent using attention seeds, depth-continuous graph connectivity, patch-mass-preserving evidence, and surface-matched robot removal, without an external segmentation network.
-\item \textbf{Separate spatial and temporal evidence.} We use internal feature memory for region identity and consistency, while geometry determines surface extent. This permits identity reassociation without interpreting a remembered descriptor as unobserved object shape.
-\item \textbf{Grounding-to-contact integration.} We specify how supported target surfaces and interaction state configure link-specific fields, attached-object queries, and constrained action-prefix execution within one AG3S pipeline.
+\item \textbf{Observed-surface grounding from a frozen VLA.} An attention-seeded, depth-continuous graph estimates target extent without an external segmentation network, using surface-matched robot removal to retain observations that a volume mask can discard.
+\item \textbf{Separate extent estimation and temporal identity.} Geometry determines surface membership, while internal feature memory supports region reassociation and consistency checks without treating remembered identity as unobserved object shape.
+\item \textbf{A task-conditioned contact representation.} Supported target surfaces and interaction state configure link-specific collision queries and attached geometry, connecting visual grounding to action-chunk refinement and prefix checking within one AG3S pipeline.
 \end{itemize}
-These contributions concern the construction and use of contact geometry; graph regularization, allowed-collision rules, and numerical trajectory optimization serve as established building blocks.
